<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leo I, King of Armenia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1150–<a href="/years/1219">1219</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Levon the Magnificent, Leo II of Armenia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubenid-dynasty">Rubenid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leo I, called the Magnificent, was the Rubenid prince of Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from 1187 (as Leo II) and its first king from 1198 to 1219. He secured a crown from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> Henry VI and Pope Celestine III and was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, turning the principality into a kingdom. He adopted Frankish feudal institutions, encouraged Italian trade and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> over its succession.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leo-i-king-of-armenia.jpg" alt="Leo I, King of Armenia" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cilician Armenia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Levon_I_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leo was born in 1150, the younger son of Stephen, third son of Leo I, lord of Armenian Cilicia, and of Rita, a daughter of Sempad, Lord of Barbaron. His father was murdered on 7 February 1165 on his way to a banquet given by the Byzantine governor of Cilicia, Andronicus Euphorbenus. Leo and his elder brother Roupen then lived with their uncle Mleh I, who had made many enemies by his cruelties and was assassinated by his own soldiers in Sis in 1175. The nobles elected Roupen III as ruler. Roupen was taken prisoner by Bohemond III, and in 1187 Leo became ruler.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1187 Leo fought a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and forced the allied forces to retreat. This drew away part of the forces facing the crusaders just as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> began his decisive battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Vahram of Edessa's Rhymed Chronicle records that he enlarged his principality, conquered Isauria, reached Iconium, took Heraclea and gave it up for a large ransom, nearly took Caesarea, and ringed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> with forts and castles. He led his forces alongside the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> and gave the crusaders provisions, guides and pack animals. He was crowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, the traditional coronation place of Cilician monarchs, on 6 January 1198 or 1199.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leo married Isabelle in 1188 or 1189 and divorced her in 1206, and married Sibylla of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> around 1210. His heir was his daughter Isabella, born around 1216. Smbat Sparapet's Chronicle describes his anger when told of the unfaithfulness of the lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, whom he had as a wife. Feeling his end near, he appointed the baron Atan regent and guardian of his daughter, and died on 2 May 1219. He was buried in the church of Agner, and part of his body was taken to Sis. Smbat describes him as wise, skilled on horseback and brave in battle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leo I, King of Armenia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1198 <b>Leo II</b> was crowned king with the blessing of the Holy Roman Emperor and the pope, transforming it into a kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/armenian-principality-of-cilicia">Armenian Principality of Cilicia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia. Died in Kozan, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.45,35.81&amp;year=1194" aria-label="Kozan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5445 816 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Leo I, King of Armenia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5895 1086h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5895 1086h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.45,35.81&amp;year=1194">What was here in 1194? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
